## Settlements are activity hubs

Settlements are heavily impacted by their surrounding geography.
They can be expected to grow or shrink in response to world events, and a history of their influence should be captured.

### Generate settlement details

The size of your settlements should have been chosen as you designed the hex map, but you may adjust their sizes on the map now, as needed.
Then, you can start detailing the bonds of the city.

Settlement bonds are recorded as tile history.
Entries should begin with:

- Name: Give the current title of the settlement as the name of the tile; this may change later.
- Origin: Who (creatures, factions, government) founded this settlement, and why?
- Resources: What resources (goods, services) do the residents deal with?
- Factions: What other groups (creatures, factions, government) have taken residence here, and why?
- Culture: What immediate impressions (ideals, language, religion, food, architecture) would travelers see when visiting?
- Events: What notable events have happened here?

Make these brief, as bullet points; leave room for you to improvise as the players engage later.
Relate these to the qualities of the nearby world.

## Factions

First, detail the values of the faction leaders.

- motivation
- obstacles

Then, detail the values of the faction followers.

- motivations

Write incongruencies between leaders and followers; conflict will emerge naturally.

## NPCs

Write incongruencies into NPC values; conflict will emerge naturally.

- name
- appearance: sensory
- personality: ideals, traits
- motivation: personal goals
- occupation: social value
- history...

